deep.reconstruction:
This page is calendrical bookkeeping, not mathematics. Gauss counts days of his own life, and every entry checks out arithmetically.

(1) Day-numbering convention (established by checking against the dated entries, my inference): day N = 30 April 1777 (Gauss's birthday) + (N-1) days, so the birthday is day 1. Anchors:
- 8113 is glossed '99 VII(I).16 D.' and works out to 16 July 1799. That is the date of Gauss's doctorate at Helmstedt, so 'D.' = Doctor/Dissertation and the month should read VII.
- 7291 is glossed 'C.d.A.' in the header and '4.15' in the date column, i.e. 15 April 1797.

(2) Main table: 23 rows plus 2 boxed rows (5343, 5397). The columns are:
(a) day number N;
(b) a count k attached to that day;
(c) a running total of k;
(d) N mod 7;
(e) the civil date (month.day). The year runs 1795 to 1797.

Column (c): the boxed rows contribute 1+2 = 3. The running total then goes 3+4=7, 11, 15, 20, 23, 30, 32, 34, 36, 38, 41, 43, 44, 46, 47, 49, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, 56. Every partial sum on the page is correct.

Column (d), all verified:
- 5343≡2, 5397≡0, 6740≡6, 6911≡2, 6928≡5, 7291≡4, 7303≡2, 7312≡4, 7336≡0;
- 7344≡1 (written as '1' in the annotation area);
- 7356≡6, 7382≡4, 7387≡2, 7393≡1, 7402≡3, 7445≡4, 7447≡6, 7448≡0, 7451≡3, 7452≡4, 7453≡5, 7471≡2, 7483≡0, 7487≡4, 7518≡0.

With day 1 a Wednesday (30 Apr 1777 was indeed a Wednesday), the residues map to weekdays as 1=Wed, 2=Thu, 3=Fri, 4=Sat, 5=Sun, 6=Mon, 0=Tue. So column (d) is the weekday of each entry.

The tally at the right (0 ....., 1 .., 2 ....., 3 .., 4 ......, 5 .., 6 ...) is the frequency of these residues over all 25 entries: 5, 2, 5, 2, 6, 2, 3 (total 25). This is exactly correct.

Column (e): converting N to dates gives the following, all matching the page except one:
- 6740 = 12 Oct 1795; 6911 = 31 Mar 1796 (Gauss writes 3.30); 6928 = 17 Apr 1796;
- 7291 = 15 Apr 1797; 7303 = 27 Apr; 7312 = 6 May; 7336 = 30 May; 7344 = 7 Jun; 7356 = 19 Jun;
- 7382 = 15 Jul; 7387 = 20 Jul; 7393 = 26 Jul; 7402 = 4 Aug;
- 7445 = 16 Sep; 7447 = 18; 7448 = 19; 7451 = 22; 7452 = 23; 7453 = 24;
- 7471 = 12 Oct; 7483 = 24 Oct; 7487 = 28 Oct; 7518 = 28 Nov 1797.

The boxed entries convert to 5343 = 15 Dec 1791 and 5397 = 7 Feb 1792.

What the counts k measure (items acquired, borrowed or sent?) and what the marks B, G, ✠, +, ⊥ and the short English remarks mean is not determinable from the page. The series starts days before his Göttingen matriculation in Oct 1795, so library loans are a possible but unproven reading.

(3) Isolated later day numbers:
- 8739 = 3 April 1801. That was a Friday (8739≡3), and Good Friday of 1801. It is marked with a circle symbol.
- 1824 block: Gauss computes 47·365 = 17155 (47 years since 1777). He then adds leap days, written as 17197 in the transcription; the correct day number for 30 Apr 1824 is 17155+11+1 = 17167 on his convention.
- 17224 = 26 Jun 1824 ('W. z. g. Z.'), 17305 = 15 Sep 1824, 17315 = 25 Sep 1824, 17405 = 24 Dec 1824 ('semb').

(4) Unexplained columns: the top column (1,1,1,2,3,4,5,6,8,9,9,12,12,17,18,24,26,31,43,54,172,363,1343) and the small pair-lists (5.3, 31.5, 43.7, 51.9, 75.11, 84.18, 96.21 and 1.2, 3.3, 46.5, 55.6, 61.8, 66.11, 92.13, 104.15, 112.17). They have no evident relation to the table, and I do not attempt to interpret them.
